---

<DocsLayout
  title="Quickstart"
  description="Get BudgetBaby working accurately in minutes. Preparation, the guided onboarding walkthrough, setting starting balances, logging your first transaction, and building a first-week habit."
  canonical="/budgetbaby/docs/quickstart"
  headings={headings}
>
  <div class="docs-content">
    <h1>Quickstart</h1>
    <p class="docs-summary">
      BudgetBaby can be set up in about 5-10 minutes. The guided onboarding walks you through
      everything the app needs to calculate Safe to Spend. The more accurate the numbers you enter,
      the more useful the daily Safe to Spend will be from day one.
    </p>

    <h2 id="before-you-open">Before you open the app</h2>
    <p>
      BudgetBaby calculates Safe to Spend from the numbers you provide. Before you start onboarding,
      have three things ready:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Your take-home pay for one pay period.</strong> This is your income after taxes and
        any pre-tax deductions. Look at your last paycheck or your bank deposit. Use the amount that
        actually hits your account, not your gross salary.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your current checking account balance.</strong> This becomes your starting Debit
        balance during onboarding's <em>Current Balances</em> step. A rough estimate is fine for
        day one, you can adjust it later.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your biggest upcoming bill.</strong> Typically rent or a mortgage payment. Knowing
        the amount and due day helps you configure it correctly when onboarding gets to the
        <em>Essentials</em> step.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      You don't need a full list of expenses up front. Onboarding pre-loads sensible defaults and
      you can add bills, categories, subscriptions, and goals at any time afterwards.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-mocks-row">
      <DocMock label="Welcome" caption="The first launch greets you with a single primary action, start onboarding.">
        <div class="dm-bar"><span></span><span class="dm-bar-action">Skip</span></div>
        <div style="text-align:center; padding:18px 4px 8px;">
          <div style="width:54px; height:54px; margin:0 auto 10px; border-radius:14px; background:linear-gradient(135deg, #8B5CF6, #6D28D9); display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; font-size:26px; font-weight:800; color:white; box-shadow:0 8px 18px -4px rgba(139,92,246,0.5);">B</div>
          <div class="dm-title" style="font-size:14px;">Welcome to BudgetBaby</div>
          <div class="dm-help" style="margin-top:4px;">Set up your budget in about 5-10 minutes. Your data stays on this device.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="dm-card" style="margin-top:6px;">
          <div class="dm-kv"><span>Your Budget</span><span class="muted">~30s</span></div>
          <div class="dm-kv"><span>Payment Methods + balances</span><span class="muted">~1m</span></div>
          <div class="dm-kv"><span>Essentials, Variable, Fun</span><span class="muted">~3m</span></div>
          <div class="dm-kv"><span>Subscriptions, savings, alerts</span><span class="muted">~2m</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="dm-btn dm-btn-primary" style="margin-top:6px;">Get Started</div>
      </DocMock>
    </div>

    <h2 id="onboarding">First launch and setup</h2>
    <p>
      The first time you open BudgetBaby, a guided setup walks you through everything the app needs
      to calculate Safe to Spend. There are sixteen short steps, grouped into five phases. Most
      steps take 15-30 seconds. The whole process is typically 5-10 minutes end to end.
    </p>

    <h3>Account &amp; profile (4 steps)</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Welcome.</strong> A brief introduction to Safe to Spend and what the setup covers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your Data.</strong> A short note explaining that BudgetBaby is local-first and that
        cloud features are opt-in.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hello!</strong> Optional: choose a display name. You can leave it blank and skip.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Welcome (continued).</strong> A summary screen that confirms you're ready to set up
        your budget.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Budget basics (3 steps)</h3>
    <ol start="5">
      <li>
        <strong>Your Budget.</strong> Enter your <strong>take-home pay per period</strong> and your
        <strong>pay frequency</strong>. Frequency options: daily, weekly, bi-weekly, twice monthly,
        or monthly. This is the single most important number in the app. Safe to Spend is
        calculated relative to this. Use take-home pay, not gross salary.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Payment Methods.</strong> Confirm which of the four pools you'll use: Cash, Debit,
        Credit, Savings. You can hide pools you don't need; the rest will show on the home screen.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Current Balances.</strong> For each enabled pool, enter what's actually in your
        account or wallet right now. This becomes the starting balance for the current period and
        carries forward automatically each period.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Categories &amp; spending (5 steps)</h3>
    <ol start="8">
      <li>
        <strong>Essentials.</strong> Non-negotiable obligations, rent, utilities, internet, phone.
        These are color-coded <strong>red</strong>. Unpaid red bills are reserved out of Safe to
        Spend automatically until you mark them paid.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Variable Needs.</strong> Important but flexible spending, groceries, transport,
        healthcare. These are color-coded <strong>yellow</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fun &amp; Lifestyle.</strong> Discretionary spending, dining out, entertainment,
        shopping, hobbies. Color-coded <strong>green</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Subscriptions.</strong> Recurring services like Netflix, Spotify, iCloud. The free
        plan tracks up to 3 subscriptions; Premium is unlimited.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Important Expenses.</strong> One-off but anticipated expenses (a wedding, a vet
        visit, an annual fee). Free plan tracks up to 3; Premium is unlimited.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Savings &amp; alerts (2 steps)</h3>
    <ol start="13">
      <li>
        <strong>Savings.</strong> Set a monthly savings target. The amount you protect here is
        reserved out of Safe to Spend automatically. The free plan supports up to 3 savings goals;
        Premium is unlimited.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Notifications.</strong> Choose whether to allow bill and renewal reminders. Default
        reminder is one day before each due date, fired at 9:00 AM. You can adjust this any time in
        Settings → Reminders.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Wrap-up (2 steps)</h3>
    <ol start="15">
      <li>
        <strong>Choose Your Plan.</strong> Stay on the free plan or start a Premium subscription.
        Skipping this keeps you on free, every core feature is available, with limits on how many
        bills, subscriptions, goals, and custom categories you can create.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>All Set!</strong> A final confirmation screen. Tap to enter the home tab and start
        budgeting.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="doc-tip">
      <strong>You can change any of this later.</strong> Settings → Budget Settings lets you edit
      income, frequency, pool visibility, and savings allocation any time. Categories and bills are
      managed from their own tabs. New settings take effect for the current and future periods;
      your existing budget history is preserved.
    </div>

    <div class="doc-mocks-row">
      <DocMock label="Step 5. Your Budget" caption="Enter take-home pay and pay frequency. Income drives every other number.">
        <div class="dm-bar"><span class="dm-back">Back</span><span class="dm-bar-title">Your Budget</span><span class="dm-bar-action">Next</span></div>
