    <h2 id="priority-colors">How priority colors work</h2>
    <p>
      Every category in BudgetBaby has a priority color. The color isn't decorative, it changes how
      the category affects your Safe to Spend.
    </p>
    <ul class="doc-status-list">
      <li>
        <span class="status-dot red"></span>
        <div>
          <strong>Essentials (red).</strong> Non-negotiable bills and necessities. Unpaid red bills
          are reserved out of Safe to Spend automatically. You physically can't see that money as
          available spending until you mark the bill paid.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="status-dot yellow"></span>
        <div>
          <strong>Variable (yellow).</strong> Important but flexible. Groceries, gas, healthcare, things you need but can adjust week-to-week if money is tight.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="status-dot green"></span>
        <div>
          <strong>Fun (green).</strong> Lifestyle and enjoyment. Dining, entertainment, shopping,
          hobbies. The first place to look when you need to cut back.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="status-dot muted"></span>
        <div>
          <strong>Unplanned (gray).</strong> Unexpected or miscellaneous. One-off costs that don't
          fit cleanly elsewhere. Gray spending doesn't affect the Bill Reserve.
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Free users get 12 default categories spread across these four colors (4 red, 3 yellow, 4 green,
      1 gray). Premium unlocks unlimited custom categories, each with its own SF Symbol icon, color,
      and name.
    </p>

    <h2 id="logging-a-transaction">Logging a transaction</h2>
    <p>
      Every purchase you log reduces Safe to Spend in real time. The faster you log, the more
      accurate the number stays. Most people develop a habit of logging immediately after paying.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Tap the <strong>+</strong> button on the home screen, or in any transactions list.</li>
      <li>Enter the <strong>amount</strong>.</li>
      <li>Pick a <strong>Category</strong>. The category determines the priority color and which
      pool the spending shows in. If nothing fits, use <em>Other</em> (gray) for now and update it
      later.</li>
      <li>Pick a <strong>Payment Method</strong>. Cash, Debit, Credit, or Savings. This determines
      which pool the transaction is logged to. Use what you actually paid with.</li>
      <li>Confirm the <strong>date</strong>. It defaults to today. Adjust it if you're logging a
      past transaction.</li>
      <li>Add an optional <strong>note</strong> ("Trader Joe's pre-dinner run"), useful when
      reviewing a month later.</li>
      <li>Tap <strong>Save</strong>. Safe to Spend updates immediately.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="doc-tip">
      <strong>Choosing the right payment method.</strong> Use Cash if you paid with physical
      currency. Use Debit for a bank card or transfer. Use Credit when you charged a credit card.
      Use Savings only when withdrawing from your savings pool toward a goal. Getting this right
      keeps the four pool balances meaningful.
    </div>
    <div class="doc-tip">
      <strong>How credit purchases affect balances.</strong> When you log a Credit transaction,
      your Debit balance does not change immediately. The real transfer from your bank happens when
      you pay the credit card bill. At that point, log a Debit → Credit transfer to keep both pool
      balances accurate.
    </div>

