    <h2 id="common-mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Skipping starting balances.</strong> The most common reason pool balances look
        wrong in week one. Open Settings → Payment Methods → Money Setup and set them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Logging credit card purchases as Debit.</strong> If you paid with a card, log it as
        Credit. Logging it as Debit reduces your Debit pool immediately, which is wrong, the
        actual debit happens when you pay the card bill.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not marking bills paid.</strong> An unpaid red bill stays reserved in Safe to
        Spend. If you paid rent but left it unpaid in BudgetBaby, Safe to Spend shows a number
        lower than reality.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Comparing Safe to Spend to your bank balance.</strong> They measure different
        things. Safe to Spend is a daily allowance from your budget. Your Debit pool is an
        approximation of your checking account. They can and will differ.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Entering gross income instead of take-home pay.</strong> BudgetBaby is built around
        take-home pay. Gross salary makes Safe to Spend appear higher than your real spending
        capacity and miscalibrates the entire system.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Skipping small purchases.</strong> Every unlogged $4 coffee is a gap in your data.
        Log even small purchases for the first month to build an accurate picture.
      </li>
    </ul>

